## Why This Is Asked

This task tests the ability to decouple algorithmic filters from execution backends, enabling runtime backend swapping and new backend integration without touching core logic.

## Key Concepts

- Bridge pattern for decoupling abstraction (filters) from implementation (backends)
- Backend interface and concrete backends (CPU, GPU)
- Extensibility and testability with pluggable backends

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal illustrative code (not production-ready)
class Backend {
  apply(image, opSpec) { throw new Error('not implemented') }
}
class CPUBackend extends Backend {
  apply(image, opSpec) { /* CPU path */ return image }
}
class GPUBackend extends Backend {
  apply(image, opSpec) { /* GPU path, e.g., CUDA/Vulkan */ return image }
}
class Filter {
  constructor(opSpec) { this.opSpec = opSpec }
  render(image, backend) { return backend.apply(image, this.opSpec) }
}
class Pipeline {
  constructor() { this.filters = []; }
  setBackend(b) { this.backend = b }
  addFilter(f) { this.filters.push(f) }
  run(image) { let out = image; for (const f of this.filters) out = f.render(out, this.backend); return out }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you add backends with different precision or memory constraints?
- How would you test backends in isolation and measure performance trade-offs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design an extensible, runtime-configurable pipeline using classic design patterns, with attention to non-blocking dispatch and clear separation of concerns.

## Key Concepts

- Strategy pattern for formatters and sinks
- Registry for runtime extension points
- Async broadcasting to sinks with fault tolerance

## Code Example

```javascript
interface LogRecord { level: string; message: string; timestamp: number; meta?: any; }
interface Formatter { format(rec: LogRecord): string; }
interface Sink { log(message: string): Promise<void>; }

class Registry<T> {
  private map = new Map<string, T>();
  register(name: string, item: T) { this.map.set(name, item); }
  get(name: string): T | undefined { return this.map.get(name); }
}

class Logger {
  private formatters = new Registry<Formatter>();
  private sinks = new Registry<Sink[]>();

  logRecord(rec: LogRecord) {
    const formatter = this.formatters.get("default");
    const msg = (formatter?.format(rec) ?? `${rec.timestamp} ${rec.level}: ${rec.message}`);
    const sinks = this.sinks.get("default") ?? [];
    Promise.all(sinks.map(s => s.log(msg))).catch(() => {});
  }

  registerFormatter(name: string, f: Formatter) { this.formatters.register(name, f); }
  registerSink(name: string, s: Sink) {
    const arr = this.sinks.get(name) ?? [];
    arr.push(s);
    this.sinks.register(name, arr);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle backpressure if sinks become slow or fail repeatedly?
- How would you hot-swap formatters without losing logs during a switch?
